---
// REVIEW: Layer 2 interpretation for the Cognitive Failures Questionnaire,
// drafted for Craig's review. Static, always visible, rendered at build time.
// FAQ answers come from cognitive-failures-questionnaire.content.ts and are
// shown word for word (they also feed the page's FAQPage JSON-LD).
import { FAQ, REFERENCE, SOURCES } from '../../tools/cognitive-failures-questionnaire.content';
import '../../styles/interpretation.css';

const s = SOURCES;
---

<!-- REVIEW -->
<section class="interpretation" aria-labelledby="understanding-cfq">
	<h2 id="understanding-cfq">Understanding your Cognitive Failures Questionnaire score</h2>

	<h3 id="what-it-measures">What the score measures</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.measures.a}</p>
	<p>
		The forgetfulness, distractibility and false triggering figures follow the three factors described by
		<a href={s.rast.href} rel="noopener">{s.rast.label}</a>, in which many items count towards two factors. An
		analysis of 839 adults found these factors so closely related that the total fitted best, and advised against
		subscales (<a href={s.goodman.href} rel="noopener">{s.goodman.label}</a>). Treat them as a rough guide.
	</p>

	<h3 id="score-bands">Score bands</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.normal.a}</p>
	<table class="interp-table">
		<caption>Average CFQ totals in published studies (number of people in brackets)</caption>
		<thead>
			<tr><th scope="col">Group</th><th scope="col">Average</th><th scope="col">Source</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			{REFERENCE.map(r => (
				<tr><td>{r.group}</td><td>{r.mean}</td><td><a href={s[r.source].href} rel="noopener">{s[r.source].label}</a></td></tr>
			))}
		</tbody>
	</table>
	<p>The studies used different recall periods, so compare loosely.</p>

	<h3 id="change">What a change in score means</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.change.a}</p>
	<p>
		Broadbent's team also found scores stable over long periods
		(<a href={s.broadbent.href} rel="noopener">{s.broadbent.label}</a>). With no published figure, one Dutch study used a
		10-point change as its own threshold (<a href={s.coflow.href} rel="noopener">{s.coflow.label}</a>): a reasonable rule of
		thumb, not a validated one.
	</p>

	<h3 id="what-it-does-not-measure">What it does not measure</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.impairment.a}</p>
	<p>
		It does not measure mental fatigue or what thinking costs you afterwards: see the
		<a href="/tools/chalder-fatigue-scale/">Chalder Fatigue Scale</a>, the <a href="/tools/dsq-pem/">DSQ-PEM</a> and the
		<a href="/evidence/post-exertional-malaise/">post-exertional malaise explainer</a>.
	</p>

	<h3 id="taking-it-to-your-gp">Taking this to your GP or consultant</h3>
	<p>
		Bring the total, the date and the trend from your saved results. Describe the CFQ as a validated 25-question measure
		of how often everyday lapses happen, widely used in long COVID research, and give two or three examples that matter,
		such as missed appointments or medication mistakes. It can support a request for a cognitive assessment or work
		adjustments; it cannot show the cause or confirm impairment. See also
		<a href="/nhs/preparing-for-gp-appointment/">preparing for a GP appointment</a>.
	</p>

	<h3 id="sources">Sources</h3>
	<ul class="interp-sources">
		<li>Broadbent DE, Cooper PF, FitzGerald P, Parkes KR. The Cognitive Failures Questionnaire (CFQ) and its correlates. <em>Br J Clin Psychol</em> 1982;21(1):1–16. <a href={s.broadbent.href} rel="noopener">DOI</a></li>
		<li>Rast P, Zimprich D, Van Boxtel M, Jolles J. Factor structure and measurement invariance of the Cognitive Failures Questionnaire across the adult life span. <em>Assessment</em> 2009;16(2):145–158. <a href={s.rast.href} rel="noopener">DOI</a> (item lists as tabulated by Goodman et al. 2022)</li>
		<li>Goodman ZT, Timpano KR, Llabre MM, Bainter SA. Revisiting the factor structure and construct validity of the Cognitive Failures Questionnaire. <em>Psychol Assess</em> 2022;34(7):671–683. <a href={s.goodman.href} rel="noopener">DOI</a></li>
		<li>Bland AR, Barraclough M, Trender WR, et al. Profiles of objective and subjective cognitive function in Post-COVID Syndrome, COVID-19 recovered, and COVID-19 naïve individuals. <em>Sci Rep</em> 2024;14:13368. <a href={s.bland.href} rel="noopener">Full text</a></li>
		<li>Holland J, Brown S, O'Flanagan S, et al. Self-reported immune status and COVID-19 associated subjective cognitive functioning in post-COVID-19 syndrome: examination of an Irish cohort. <em>Brain Behav</em> 2024;14(9):e70027. <a href={s.holland.href} rel="noopener">Full text</a></li>
		<li>Miskowiak KW, Pedersen JK, Gunnarsson DV, et al. Cognitive impairments among patients in a long-COVID clinic: prevalence, pattern and relation to illness severity, work function and quality of life. <em>J Affect Disord</em> 2023;324:162–169. <a href={s.miskowiak.href} rel="noopener">Full text</a></li>
		<li>Attree EA, Arroll MA, Dancey CP, et al. Psychosocial factors involved in memory and cognitive failures in people with myalgic encephalomyelitis/chronic fatigue syndrome. <em>Psychol Res Behav Manag</em> 2014;7:67–76. <a href={s.attree.href} rel="noopener">Full text</a></li>
		<li>Berentschot JC, Bek LM, Drost M, et al. Health outcomes up to 3 years and post-exertional malaise in patients after hospitalization for COVID-19: a multicentre prospective cohort study (CO-FLOW). <em>Lancet Reg Health Eur</em> 2025;53:101290. <a href={s.coflow.href} rel="noopener">Full text</a></li>
		<li>Bridger RS, et al. Psychometric properties of the Cognitive Failures Questionnaire. <em>Ergonomics</em> 2013;56(10):1515–1524. <a href={s.bridger.href} rel="noopener">DOI</a></li>
	</ul>
</section>
